1. CMS and other federal public data

The tables in data/gold/ are derived from files published by the Centers for
Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS): the IPPS final rule tables, the Medicare
Physician Fee Schedule RVU files, the OPPS and ASC payment addenda, the Medicare
provider utilization and payment public use files (inpatient, outpatient, and
physician and other practitioners), and the hospital cost reports (HCRIS). The
ICD-10-CM code lists come from the National Center for Health Statistics at the
Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. These are U.S. federal public data.
Where CMS no longer hosts a file, the extractors can retrieve a copy from the
NBER mirror (data.nber.org) or the Wayback Machine. No CMS or CDC file is
redistributed in its original form; this repository holds only derived tables.

2. CPT

CPT is a registered trademark of the American Medical Association. CPT code
numbers appear in data/gold/code_costs.parquet because the CMS payment files are
keyed by them. The gold tables contain no CPT code descriptors. Anyone who needs
descriptors must obtain them from the American Medical Association under its
own license.

3. CMS MS-DRG Java grouper and its dependencies

The CMS MS-DRG Java grouper (versions V40.0 to V43.1) is third-party software
published by CMS. It is not distributed in this repository and is used under
CMS's own terms. The same applies to the dependencies the grouper driver needs
at run time, which the code downloads from their publishers and builds or
unpacks locally: gfc-base-api (built from the source archive named in
src/config.py), protobuf-java 3.25.5 and slf4j 1.7.36 (slf4j-api and slf4j-nop).
Each keeps its own license. The CMS packages include a file named
OpenSourceLicenseDisclosures.pdf that lists the third-party components bundled
with them. See src/grouper/jars/README.md.

Two gold tables are outputs of grouper software run on synthetic claims:
data/gold/icd_principal_drg_modal.parquet and
data/gold/icd_principal_drg_reachability.parquet. The script that builds them,
src/transform/icd_drg.py, takes each ICD-10-CM code in the code table (built
from the CDC code lists) as the principal diagnosis of a synthetic claim and
groups five fixed variants of that claim: a baseline, two with one added
secondary diagnosis, one with one added ICD-10-PCS procedure code, and one with
sex set to male (age 65 and discharge disposition 01 throughout, with a newborn
override for codes that begin with P). The tables record the MS-DRGs that the
grouper returns. For fiscal years 2023 to 2026 the grouper is the CMS MS-DRG
Java grouper (V40.0 to V43.0, the code's default version for each year). For
fiscal years 2019 to 2022 it is the drgpy package (v36 to v39), whose package
metadata gives its license as Apache-2.0. The inputs are code combinations, not
patient data, and the tables contain no grouper software and no CMS grouper
file.

The Java source file src/grouper/driver/KGCostGrouperDriver.java starts with its
own SPDX header (CC0-1.0). It is this project's own driver and calls the CMS
grouper through its Java API. That header is left as it is.

4. Python dependencies

KG-COST depends on other open-source Python packages, none of which is
redistributed here. Each is installed from PyPI and keeps its own license.
Required: requests, lxml, pandas, pyarrow, duckdb, openpyxl, python-dateutil,
xlrd (below version 2) and PyYAML. Optional, installed with the dev extra:
pytest, ruff, mypy, drgpy, pyreadstat, the neo4j Python driver and
python-dotenv. The exact requirements are in pyproject.toml.
drgpy is a third-party pure-Python MS-DRG grouper that the code uses for fiscal
years 2019 to 2022. Its package metadata (version 0.2.1) gives its license as
Apache-2.0, and it keeps that license; consult it before redistributing drgpy.

The optional Neo4j graph layer starts the neo4j:5.20-community Docker image with
the APOC plugin through neo4j/docker-compose.yml. Both are pulled from their
publishers and used under their own licenses. Nothing from them is included in
this repository.

5. Restricted data

No National Trauma Data Bank (NTDB), MIMIC-IV or other restricted-access data
are included in this repository, and none are needed to run the code. Optional
code paths that read such data (src/query/_ntdb_adapter.py,
src/query/run_phase14c.py, and the MIMIC-IV validation tests in
tests/test_grouper_validation.py and tests/test_drgpy_cross.py) take the
location of a user's own copy from an environment variable or a command-line
argument; the MIMIC-IV tests skip when it is not set. The test fixtures under
tests/fixtures/ are synthetic.

